---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionIntro from "../../components/SectionIntro.astro";
import { basicsCards } from "../../data/content";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TCM Basics | HerbGuide"
  description="Learn core Traditional Chinese Medicine ideas in clear, careful English without overpromising or overcomplicating."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">TCM Basics</p>
      <h1>Core ideas in plain English</h1>
      <p>
        We translate Chinese medicine concepts into practical explanations while keeping
        clear boundaries between traditional theory and modern medical advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionIntro
        title="Start here"
        text="These pages are designed to reduce confusion for new readers and support internal linking across herb and recipe content."
      />
      <div class="card-grid">
        {basicsCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h2>{card.title}</h2>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Plain-English frame</p>
        <h2>What TCM means on this site</h2>
        <p>
          Traditional Chinese Medicine, often shortened to TCM, is a long-running body of
          ideas about the body, daily habits, food, herbs, climate, and patterns of
          imbalance. It has its own vocabulary, and that vocabulary does not always match
          modern biomedical language one-to-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          On HerbGuide, we treat TCM as a cultural and educational framework. We explain
          how a traditional herb is described, where that language comes from, and how food
          therapy is commonly discussed in Chinese tradition. We do not present those ideas
          as personalized treatment instructions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Important boundary</p>
        <h2>How to read traditional terms responsibly</h2>
        <p>
          Words like <strong>qi</strong>, <strong>dampness</strong>, <strong>yin</strong>,
          and <strong>heat</strong> are best understood as parts of a traditional model.
          They are not direct disease labels, lab values, or guaranteed explanations for
          symptoms.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>`Qi` is often explained as functional energy, activity, or vitality.</li>
          <li>`Blood` in TCM is broader than literal blood in a lab report.</li>
          <li>`Dampness` usually describes heaviness, sluggishness, or turbid accumulation in traditional theory.</li>
          <li>`Channels` are functional pathways in TCM, not the same thing as anatomical organs or blood vessels.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What does qi mean in Traditional Chinese Medicine?</h2>
        <p>
          `Qi` is one of the first Chinese medicine words many readers encounter, and it is
          also one of the easiest to misunderstand. A practical translation is
          <em>functional vitality</em> or the body's capacity to do what it needs to do:
          warm, move, transform, protect, and recover.
        </p>
        <p>
          When TCM writing says a herb may support qi, the site will usually translate that
          into everyday language such as stamina, appetite, recovery, resilience, or general
          strength, while still making it clear that this is traditional language rather
          than a medical claim.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What are warming and cooling foods in TCM?</h2>
        <p>
          Warming and cooling are traditional qualities, not temperature alone. A cooling
          herb can be served warm as tea. A warming ingredient can appear in a room
          temperature dish. The point is the <em>traditional effect profile</em>, not simply
          whether the food is hot or cold to the touch.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why ginger, chrysanthemum, mint, and pear often get discussed so
          differently in Chinese food therapy. HerbGuide will keep translating these labels
          into simple, practical language instead of assuming the reader already knows the
          system.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What does dampness mean in Chinese medicine?</h2>
        <p>
          `Dampness` is a classic example of a TCM term that sounds strange in English.
          It does not literally mean the body is wet. In plain English, it often points to
          patterns of heaviness, stickiness, cloudiness, sluggish digestion, or a sense that
          things are not transforming smoothly.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why grains, soups, aromatic peels, and certain lighter foods often appear
          in traditional discussions of dampness. The site will keep that language general
          and educational rather than turning it into treatment advice.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>What does channel entry mean for Chinese herbs?</h2>
        <p>
          When a herb is said to enter the Lung, Spleen, or Liver channel, that does not
          mean it only affects those literal organs in a modern anatomical sense. In TCM,
          organ names often represent broader functional networks.
        </p>
        <p>
          For example, the TCM `Spleen` system has a lot to do with transformation of food,
          fluids, and daily nourishment. The TCM `Liver` system often appears in discussions
          of flow, tension, and stored blood. HerbGuide will explain these terms directly on
          herb pages so readers are not left guessing.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Search intent</p>
        <h2>Traditional Chinese Medicine terms explained in simple English</h2>
        <p>
          This page is designed to rank for practical learning searches such as Traditional
          Chinese Medicine terms explained, what qi means in simple English, what dampness
          means in TCM, and how to understand warming and cooling foods without clinical
          jargon.
        </p>
        <p>
          That search intent matches the real purpose of HerbGuide: help readers understand
          the language first so they can read herb and food therapy content more confidently.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Next steps</p>
        <h2>How these basics connect to herb pages</h2>
        <p>
          Once a reader understands qi, dampness, warming, cooling, and channel entry, the
          herb library becomes much easier to navigate. Terms on pages for astragalus, goji
          berry, chrysanthemum, jujube, ginger, and poria stop looking mysterious and start
          looking like a consistent vocabulary.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
